1
00:00:00,940 --> 00:00:09,900
A Place of Our Own

2
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:12,100
Ellen, I'd like your opinion.

3
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:14,600
About what?

4
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:20,070
Well, Richard and I feel that with a baby coming

5
00:00:20,070 --> 00:00:26,500
we need to have our own place to live.

6
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:27,938
Oh.

7
00:00:27,938 --> 00:00:29,333
Well, what do you think about Richard and me

8
00:00:29,333 --> 00:00:32,000
looking for a small house or an apartment

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,800
at this point in our lives?

10
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:37,500
We love having you here,

11
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:38,391
and there is room,

12
00:00:38,391 --> 00:00:41,300
and ... and when the baby comes,

13
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:44,873
the baby can stay in your room for a while.

14
00:00:44,873 --> 00:00:51,700
Richard feels we need to find a small house.

15
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:56,600
I remember when I was pregnant with Richard.

16
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:58,100
Philip and I were living with Grandma and Grandpa.

17
00:00:58,100 --> 00:00:59,214
Philip was a young doctor,

18
00:00:59,214 --> 00:01:03,900
and he kept talking about having a house of our own.

19
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:04,700
It's natural.

20
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:07,268
What did you do?

21
00:01:07,268 --> 00:01:10,200
We looked at a lot of houses.

22
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,200
Did you find one?

23
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:12,400
Oh, not at first.

24
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:14,300
We couldn't afford it.

25
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:18,800
Grandpa wanted to lend us the money to buy one,

26
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:20,622
but Philip is too independent.

27
00:01:20,622 --> 00:01:24,300
He didn't want to borrow any money.

28
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:26,000
Sounds like Richard.

29
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,000
They're all alike.

30
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,800
Richard is a real Stewart.

31
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:31,100
He's independent,

32
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:36,900
and sometimes just stubborn.

33
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,400
When did you buy a house?

34
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:42,600
After Richard was born.

35
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:43,500
I was teaching music,

36
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:48,400
and Philip was opening his first medical office.

37
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:49,192
Where was the house?

38
00:01:49,192 --> 00:01:51,043
Right here in Riverdale.

39
00:01:51,043 --> 00:01:54,911
Of course, it was a small house,

40
00:01:54,911 --> 00:01:58,278
but just right for us.

41
00:01:58,278 --> 00:02:00,547
It's funny. History repeats itself.

42
00:02:00,547 --> 00:02:01,900
Now Richard and I are having a baby,

43
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:03,100
and we probably won't be able to

44
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:05,988
afford a house right away, either.

45
00:02:05,988 --> 00:02:09,900
Why don't you look at some houses, Marilyn?

46
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:11,900
Good idea. \u597D\u4E3B\u610F\u3002

47
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:15,500
Look in the real-estate section of Sunday's Times.

48
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:16,600
You'll learn a lot.

49
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:18,000
Maybe we should

50
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,700
speak to a real-estate agent about a house.

51
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:23,800
And a bank about a mortgage.

52
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:25,732
I'll talk to Richard about it.

53
00:02:25,732 --> 00:02:27,200
I think it's a good idea, Ellen.

54
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:30,200
We can learn a lot by asking.

55
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:32,112
And if I can be of any help,

56
00:02:32,112 --> 00:02:33,014
let me know.

57
00:02:33,014 --> 00:02:33,700
As a matter of fact,

58
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:36,500
my friend Virginia Martinelli is a real-estate agent.

59
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:37,900
Good.

60
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:38,700
You won't believe this,

61
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:43,700
but she sold us our first house and this one.

62
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:43,900
Well, I'll tell Richard,

63
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:51,846
and we'll go to see her.

64
00:02:51,846 --> 00:02:55,854
Do you think the skirt length is right, Ellen?

65
00:02:55,854 --> 00:02:58,900
Do you think it's too long?

66
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:01,900
I think the skirt is just right.

67
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:04,500
Are you planning to attach a train to it?

68
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:07,638
No. No train. Just the dress.

69
00:03:07,638 --> 00:03:11,600
But I am going to make a headpiece of lace.

70
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:14,600
That dress is gorgeous.

71
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:16,200
Thanks, Ellen.

72
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:19,100
And thanks for the advice about the house.

73
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:21,495
I'll talk to Richard about it

74
00:03:21,495 --> 00:03:22,000
the minute he comes home.

75
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:23,800
And remember,

76
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:26,461
we love having you here.

77
00:03:26,461 --> 00:03:29,461
There's no need to rush.
